  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStill quite a bit of uncertainty with the forecast today. \nLingering morning showers as a warm front tracks across the area\nmay limit instability for early this afternoon as a prefrontal \ntrough pushes in from the west. If the warm front can get \nthrough the area, then this will allow for some clearing skies \nwithin the warm sector and the longer the break between morning \nshowers and afternoon storms will maximize instability. Forecast\nsoundings are still indicating potential for CAPE of 1500+ \nJ/kg, 30 - 35 KT of 0-6km bulk shear and PWATs increasing above \n1.5\". Also, short Corfidi vectors indicate potential for \ntraining thunderstorms and localized flash flooding may become \na concern late afternoon into this evening, especially across \nthe southern Catskills. \n\nThe main show this afternoon will be with the forcing from the \nprefrontal trough, as latest CAMs show showers and thunderstorms\ndeveloping around mid afternoon and moving across the area \nthrough early this evening. The cold front will not push through\nthe region until early Friday morning and it likely won't \nproduce much shower or storm activity as it passes. As far as \nsevere thunderstorms for this afternoon and evening, there will \nbe plenty shear, but the question will be how much instability \nwill develop before storms kick off this afternoon. Also, with \nlittle to no CIN in place to hold initiation off until a little \nlater in the afternoon will further hinder stronger instability \nfrom developing.\n\nDamaging wind gusts will be the main threat, but can't rule \nisolated large hail and an isolated weak tornado. A Slight Risk \nis still in place from SPC for all but the far western portion \nof the BGM forecast area, where a Marginal Risk for severe \nthunderstorms is in place. \n\nAlso, a Marginal Risk of excessive rainfall is in place for the\nentire area from WPC. A Slight Risk is in place across the \nsoutheastern portion of the forecast area where training of \ncells appears more likely as well as slightly higher PWAT \nvalues.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure crosses the region Friday into the weekend. This \nwill bring mostly dry and quiet weather. The only caveat to this\nis the upper trough axis remains overhead on Friday and diurnal\nheating could produce a few quick passing showers.\n\nTemperatures will cool a few degrees on Friday and Saturday, \nbut warmer temperatures and increasing humidity will return for \nSunday. Finally, with recent rainfall and Friday and Saturday \nnights expected to be clear, cool and calm, the ingredients will\nbe ripe for river valley fog across the region.",
